            The Sperrstelle Urdorf (Fortification No. 1162 / 5050) is a well-preserved World War II defensive roadblock located in the Canton of Zurich.
            Constructed primarily between 1939 and 1940, it was activated under General Guisan&apos;s Operationsbefehl Nr. 2 as a vital anchor point of the <a href="/limmatstellung" target="_blank" class="accent">Limmatstellung</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
            This defensive line served as Switzerland&apos;s primary shield against a potential invasion from the north.
            Because the three-kilometer gap between the Uetliberg, Waldegg, and the Urdorf depression was a critical bottleneck (passage obligé), the Swiss Army&apos;s 6th Division designated it as a high-priority tactical sector.
        </p>
